[THOUGHT] Valuable new information: lesions are **painless**, **non-bleeding**, **non-oozing**, and **unresponsive to prior syphilis treatment (likely penicillin) and eczema therapy (e.g., topical steroids)**. This strongly argues against primary syphilis (which typically responds to penicillin) and common inflammatory dermatoses. Painless, crusted, persistent genital + extra-genital lesions raise concern for:  
- **Cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma (SCC)** — especially in sun-exposed or chronically inflamed/scarred skin; finger lesion could be actinic or HPV-related, penile lesion may be related to phimosis, lichen sclerosus, or HPV;  
- **Cutaneous T-cell lymphoma (e.g., mycosis fungoides)** — can present with chronic, scaly, crusted plaques at multiple sites;  
- **Chronic viral infection (e.g., HPV subtypes 16/18 causing bowenoid papulosis or SCC in situ)**;  
- **Granulomatous disease (e.g., sarcoidosis, leishmaniasis)** — though less likely without systemic symptoms or travel history.  

Next, I must address domain 3: **Associated symptoms / focused review of systems**, particularly for malignancy or systemic illness (e.g., lymphadenopathy, fatigue, fevers — already denied — but also skin changes elsewhere, nail dystrophy, oral ulcers, joint pain). Also need to ask about genital-specific symptoms (urethral discharge, dysuria) to rule out coexisting STI.

[QUERY] Have you noticed any other skin changes — like new moles, sores, rashes, or patches anywhere else on your body? Any swollen lymph nodes — especially in the groin, armpits, or neck? And have you had any urinary symptoms, like discharge from the penis, burning when urinating, or frequent urination?

[THOUGHT] HIV is ruled out — this makes aggressive HPV-driven neoplasia or opportunistic infection less likely, but does not exclude immunosuppression from other causes (e.g., undiagnosed lymphoma, iatrogenic) or sporadic SCC. The clinical picture remains highly suspicious for cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma (SCC) given:  
- Two indurated, crusted, oozing, irregular-bordered, painless, non-healing lesions at disparate sites (penis + finger);  
- Resistance to prior syphilis treatment and topical steroids;  
- No systemic symptoms or lymphadenopathy.  

Other considerations — though less likely — include:  
- **Bowen disease (SCC in situ)** — especially on penis (often related to HPV) or sun-damaged finger;  
- **Cutaneous T-cell lymphoma (mycosis fungoides)** — can present with chronic, scaly, crusted plaques, though multi-site involvement without pruritus is atypical;  
- **Chronic granulomatous disease** — but no systemic features or immunodeficiency history.  

To narrow further, the most discriminative next test is **histopathologic evaluation**, which is definitive for SCC vs. inflammatory or lymphoproliferative conditions. While biopsy isn’t explicitly listed as “available” yet, it is the standard of care and almost certainly accessible. I will request it now — and if unavailable, the Analyst will specify alternatives.

[TEST] Punch biopsy of the penile lesion (preferred site due to diagnostic yield and clinical concern) for histopathology with H&E staining.

[THOUGHT] The biopsy result — “nuclear hyperchromasia, multinucleation, and increased mitotic figures within the follicle-bearing epithelium” — is highly abnormal and diagnostic of dysplastic or neoplastic squamous epithelium. While not yet explicitly labeled “SCC” or “Bowen disease”, these features are hallmark cytologic signs of squamous intraepithelial neoplasia (SIN), ranging from high-grade dysplasia to SCC in situ (Bowen disease) or invasive SCC. The location “within the follicle-bearing epithelium” suggests involvement of hair-bearing skin — consistent with penile shaft (which has sparse follicles) or finger (non-follicular, so this phrasing may reflect sampling artifact or adjacent adnexal structures). Critically, this finding confirms a neoplastic process and rules out infection, inflammation, or benign hyperplasia. No further testing is needed to establish the *nature* of the lesion — it is unequivocally premalignant or malignant squamous epithelium. All eight domains have now been addressed:  
1) Chief complaint & onset — non-healing crusted lesions;  
2) Course — persistent, painless, treatment-resistant;  
3) ROS — no systemic symptoms, no other skin lesions;  
4) PMH — no immunosuppression, autoimmune, or prior genital disease;  
5) Meds/allergies — none reported;  
6) Social/exposures — no travel, occupational hazards, or new sexual partners;  
7) Physical exam — indurated, irregular, crusted, oozing, non-tender plaques;  
8) Testing — HIV negative, biopsy shows definitive squamous dysplasia/neoplasia.  

The evidence is sufficient for conclusive diagnosis. I may now issue [FINISH].

[FINISH]

